Rewrite Medical Markdown Files

This is a script that turns medical textbook chapters, imperfectly converted
from PDF, into tagged `.md` study documents. The output files are formatted
specifically for use by the pipeline listed in

  https://github.com/medical-uc/knowledge-graph-pipeline

A textbook chapter is already finished prose, so the script does not rewrite
it. Every content paragraph in the source is carried over verbatim into a
`<con>`, with only the mechanical repairs the PDF extractor makes necessary:
non-breaking spaces and stand-in soft hyphens are normalised, and paragraphs
split mid-sentence at a page break are rejoined. Figures and tables are
emitted at the position they held in the source, and mentions of them in the
prose become inline `<figref>` and `<tblref>` references.

Inference is confined to the document metadata, which the source has no
counterpart for. In practice that is a single call for `<topic>`, plus a
caption for the occasional unnumbered inline diagram. Everything else, the
title, objectives, summary, glossary and reading list, comes from the chapter
itself. Because so little is inferred, `--offline` produces a complete and
correct document with no back end at all.

When it is used, the back end is hosted on

  http://jupyter-02.aml1.id.iosda.org:8888

through vLLM running the model `huatuogpt-o1-72b-4bit`. The Hugging Face path
of the model is `mlx-community/HuatuoGPT-o1-72B-4bit`.

The file `TAG_SCHEMA.txt` specifies all the tags and what each of them
contains. Regenerate it with `python3 rewrite_medical_md.py --print-schema`.

To run it, simply use:

  python3 rewrite_medical_md.py raw_document.md \
    -o rewritten_document.md \
    -v \
    --cache-dir ./artifacts/rewrite_cache

To process multiple documents at once, use the `batch_process.sh` script. Put
the input documents under `./inputs`, organised by subject and book:

  inputs/biochemistry/harpers_illustrated_biochemistry/chapter_1.md

The script walks that tree, mirrors its structure into `./artifacts/outputs`,
and passes any extra arguments straight through, so `./batch_process.sh
--offline` runs the whole corpus without touching the network.

Every run checks that no source paragraph was lost, reporting any that were as
warnings on stderr and exiting non-zero.
